IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cpf-withdrawal`

-- Central Provident Fund Act 1953 (Singapore) -- s 7, s 16 and s 25(1).
--
-- Three things the first pass left out, one of which CORRECTS it.
--
-- s 16 is the correction. cpf-withdrawal.l4 computed what a member may take out
-- of "the sum standing to the member's credit" without excluding the MEDISAVE
-- ACCOUNT. s 16 ring-fences that account against the ordinary withdrawal routes,
-- so the earlier figure was too high for every member with a medisave balance --
-- which is every member. The rules below supersede it, and the older rule is
-- left in place and renamed rather than deleted, so the difference is visible.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No
-- pipeline, no independent test pass, no human gate.
--
-- SCOPE REMAINS SMALL. The CPF Act is about 911,000 characters. This adds the
-- contribution duty (s 7), the medisave restrictions (s 16) and the capacity to
-- nominate (s 25(1)), and nothing else. The First Schedule contribution rates,
-- s 13 (crediting into the three accounts), s 15AA, the housing and investment
-- charges and the whole of Parts 3A onwards are still not encoded.

§ `Central Provident Fund Act 1953 -- sections 7, 16 and 25`

§§ `16 -- The medisave account is ring-fenced`

-- s 16(1): "A member of the Fund is NOT ENTITLED to withdraw any sum standing
-- to the credit of the member in the member's MEDISAVE ACCOUNT UNDER
-- SECTION 15(2)(a) OR (3)."
-- s 16(2): nor under s 15AA(1), "EXCEPT WITH THE MINISTER'S APPROVAL."
-- s 16(3): "The Board MUST NOT ALLOW" a withdrawal from the medisave account
-- under s 15(4).
--
-- READ THE LIST OF ROUTES IT NAMES. s 16(1) restricts withdrawal under
-- s 15(2)(a) -- reaching 55 -- and under s 15(3) -- the annual further
-- withdrawal. It does NOT name s 15(2)(b), the route for a member who is not an
-- applicable person. See the note at the foot of this file.
DECLARE Accounts HAS
    `the total sum standing to the member's credit` IS A NUMBER
    `the sum standing to the credit of the medisave account` IS A NUMBER

-- The part of the credit that the ordinary routes can reach at all.
GIVEN `the accounts` IS AN Accounts
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the credit outside the medisave account` IS
    `the accounts`'s `the total sum standing to the member's credit`
        MINUS `the accounts`'s `the sum standing to the credit of the medisave account`

DECLARE WithdrawalRoute IS ONE OF
    `section 15(2)(a) -- having attained 55`
    `section 15(2)(b) -- not being an applicable person`
    `section 15(3) -- the annual further withdrawal`
    `section 15(4) -- at the Board's discretion`
    `section 15AA(1)`

GIVEN `the route` IS A WithdrawalRoute, `the Minister has approved` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the medisave account may be withdrawn by this route` IF
    CONSIDER `the route`
    WHEN `section 15(2)(a) -- having attained 55` THEN FALSE          -- s 16(1)
    WHEN `section 15(3) -- the annual further withdrawal` THEN FALSE  -- s 16(1)
    WHEN `section 15(4) -- at the Board's discretion` THEN FALSE      -- s 16(3)
    WHEN `section 15AA(1)` THEN `the Minister has approved`           -- s 16(2)
    OTHERWISE TRUE                                                     -- s 15(2)(b)

-- The sum a given route can reach, once s 16 is applied.
GIVEN `the accounts` IS AN Accounts
    , `the route` IS A WithdrawalRoute
    , `the Minister has approved` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the sum this route can reach` IS
    IF   `the medisave account may be withdrawn by this route` `the route` `the Minister has approved`
    THEN `the accounts`'s `the total sum standing to the member's credit`
    ELSE `the credit outside the medisave account` `the accounts`

§§ `The corrected withdrawal figure`

-- s 15 decides WHETHER money may come out and how much must be set aside;
-- s 16 decides WHICH ACCOUNT it may come out of. Both have to be applied, and
-- the first pass applied only the first.
--
-- The retirement sum under s 15(6) is set aside from the credit after excluding
-- the reserved amount; the medisave balance is simply unavailable on these
-- routes. So the figure is computed on the non-medisave credit throughout.
DECLARE MemberAccounts HAS
    `the member`        IS A Member
    `the accounts`      IS AN Accounts
    `the Minister has approved a section 15AA(1) medisave withdrawal` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the holding` IS A MemberAccounts
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the reachable credit on the 55 route` IS
    `the credit outside the medisave account` (`the holding`'s `the accounts`)

GIVEN `the holding` IS A MemberAccounts
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the amount withdrawable at 55 after section 16 and the set-aside` IS
    IF   `the reachable credit on the 55 route` `the holding`
             AT LEAST `the holding`'s `the member`'s `the prescribed retirement sum applicable to the member`
    THEN `the reachable credit on the 55 route` `the holding`
             MINUS `the holding`'s `the member`'s `the prescribed retirement sum applicable to the member`
    ELSE 0

-- The member who is not an applicable person is on s 15(2)(b), which s 16 does
-- not restrict and s 15(6) does not reach. They take everything, medisave
-- included.
GIVEN `the holding` IS A MemberAccounts
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the amount withdrawable on ceasing to be an applicable person` IS
    `the holding`'s `the accounts`'s `the total sum standing to the member's credit`

GIVEN `the holding` IS A MemberAccounts
    , `the Board has given its authority` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the amount this member may actually withdraw` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the Board has given its authority` THEN 0
        IF `the member is entitled to withdraw under section 15(2)(b)` (`the holding`'s `the member`)
            THEN `the amount withdrawable on ceasing to be an applicable person` `the holding`
        IF `the member is entitled to withdraw under section 15(2)(a)` (`the holding`'s `the member`)
            THEN `the amount withdrawable at 55 after section 16 and the set-aside` `the holding`
        OTHERWISE 0

§§ `7 -- The contribution duty`

-- s 7(1): "EVERY employer of an employee MUST pay to the Fund MONTHLY in
-- respect of each employee contributions at the appropriate rates set out in
-- the FIRST SCHEDULE."
--
-- The rates are in the First Schedule and vary by age and wage band. They are
-- not encoded: like Tables A and B of the Work Injury Compensation Act, they
-- are a long table crossing page breaks, and the same extraction caution
-- applies. The rate is therefore a supplied fact.
--
-- s 7(1A): the Board may authorise payment "at other intervals NOT EXCEEDING
-- 6 MONTHS."
-- s 7(2): "DESPITE the provisions of any written law or any contract to the
-- contrary", the employer may recover from the employee's monthly wages the
-- amount the First Schedule shows as recoverable.
-- s 7(3): an employer who HAS RECOVERED the employee's share and then fails to
-- pay it to the Fund in time commits an offence -- a fine not exceeding $10,000
-- or imprisonment not exceeding SEVEN YEARS or both.
`the maximum interval the Board may authorise, in months` MEANS 6
`the maximum fine under section 7(3) in dollars` MEANS 10000
`the maximum imprisonment under section 7(3) in years` MEANS 7

DECLARE ContributionFacts HAS
    `the employer's contribution rate from the First Schedule` IS A NUMBER
    `the amount shown in the First Schedule as recoverable from the employee` IS A NUMBER
    `the employee's monthly wages`              IS A NUMBER
    `the interval authorised by the Board, in months` IS A NUMBER
    `the employer recovered the employee's share from the wages` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employer paid the contributions to the Fund within the prescribed time` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the facts` IS A ContributionFacts
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the contribution payable to the Fund` IS
    `the facts`'s `the employee's monthly wages`
        TIMES `the facts`'s `the employer's contribution rate from the First Schedule`

GIVEN `the facts` IS A ContributionFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the authorised interval is within section 7(1A)` IF
    `the facts`'s `the interval authorised by the Board, in months`
        AT MOST `the maximum interval the Board may authorise, in months`

-- s 7(3) is narrower than a general failure-to-pay offence: it bites only where
-- the employer HAS ALREADY TAKEN the money out of the employee's wages. An
-- employer who never recovered the employee's share and never paid its own
-- contributions is outside this subsection.
GIVEN `the facts` IS A ContributionFacts
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an offence under section 7(3) is committed` IF
        `the facts`'s `the employer recovered the employee's share from the wages`
    AND NOT `the facts`'s `the employer paid the contributions to the Fund within the prescribed time`

§§ `25(1) -- Who may nominate`

-- s 25(1): "any member of the Fund (BEING AN APPLICABLE PERSON) who is AT LEAST
-- 16 YEARS OF AGE may, by a memorandum executed in such manner as the Board may
-- prescribe, nominate any person to receive in his or her own right ..."
--
-- Two conditions on the member: an applicable person, and 16 or over. Note the
-- first -- a member who has ceased to be a citizen or permanent resident may
-- withdraw the whole Fund under s 15(2)(b) but may NOT make a nomination.
`the minimum age to nominate` MEANS 16

-- The Member record in cpf-withdrawal.l4 carries only the one age fact s 15
-- needs -- whether the member has attained 55 -- so whether they have attained
-- 16 is taken as its own input rather than inferred from it. Inferring it would
-- make every member under 55 ineligible to nominate, which is not the law.
GIVEN `the member` IS A Member
    , `the member has attained 16 years of age` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the member may make a nomination under section 25(1)` IF
        `the member is an applicable person` `the member`
    AND `the member has attained 16 years of age`

-- s 25(1)(a)(ii)(B): where the nominated portion "EXCEEDS SUCH MAXIMUM AMOUNT
-- AS THE MINISTER MAY DETERMINE", the maximum is transferred into the nominee's
-- own CPF accounts and the EXCESS is paid out to them. So a large nomination to
-- a citizen or permanent resident is split in two.
GIVEN `the portion nominated` IS A NUMBER
    , `the maximum amount the Minister has determined` IS A NUMBER
    , `the nominee is a citizen or permanent resident of Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the amount transferred into the nominee's own accounts` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the nominee is a citizen or permanent resident of Singapore` THEN 0
        IF `the portion nominated` GREATER THAN `the maximum amount the Minister has determined`
            THEN `the maximum amount the Minister has determined`
        OTHERWISE `the portion nominated`

GIVEN `the portion nominated` IS A NUMBER
    , `the maximum amount the Minister has determined` IS A NUMBER
    , `the nominee is a citizen or permanent resident of Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `the amount paid out to the nominee` IS
    `the portion nominated`
        MINUS `the amount transferred into the nominee's own accounts`
                  `the portion nominated`
                  `the maximum amount the Minister has determined`
                  `the nominee is a citizen or permanent resident of Singapore`
